{{also|runup|run-up}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-au-run up.ogg|Audio (AU)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|runs up|running up|ran up|run up}}

# {{&lit|run|up|lang=en}}
#: ''The small boy '''ran up''' the hill.''
# To hasten to a destination.
#: ''The dog '''ran up''' under the table to get his food.''
# To erect hastily, as a building.
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[make]] something, usually an item of clothing, very [[quickly]].
#: ''I'll '''run''' you '''up''' a skirt for tomorrow evening.''
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To bring a [[flag]] to the top of its [[flag pole]].
#: ''Stand quietly while the honor guard '''runs''' the flag '''up'''.''
# {{lb|en|cricket}} of a [[bowler]], to run, or [[walk]] up to the [[bowling crease]] in order to [[bowl]] a [[ball]].
# To rise; to swell; to grow; to increase.
#: ''Accounts of goods credited '''run up''' very fast.''
#* Sir Walter Scott
#*: But these, having been untrimmed for many years, had '''run up''' into great bushes, or rather dwarf trees.
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[accumulate]] a [[debt]].
#: ''He '''ran up''' over $5,000 in unpaid bills.''
# {{lb|en|figurative}} To thrust up, as anything long and slender.
#: The fence '''runs up''' along the edge of the pasture.

